 must converge to the function.' Which correction matches the definition?","options":["Taylor polynomials are unrelated to derivatives.","Taylor polynomials exist only for polynomials.","The full Taylor series never converges to any function.","A Taylor polynomial does not ensure the series converges to $f$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"76f8f6d1c5063d1f42dd0cd1","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is $P_k$ described as an asymptotic best fit rather than merely any polynomial of degree $k$?","options":["Because it has integer coefficients.","It matches $f$'s derivatives at $a$ through order $k$.","Because it equals $f$ at each point of the entire domain.","Because it ignores the value $f(a)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bc3c44855dd1b2328310f559","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Which polynomial could be the $2$nd order Taylor polynomial at $a = 0$ for a function with $f(0) = 1$, $f'(0) = 2$, and $f''(0) = 6$?","options":["$1+2x+3x^2$","$1+6x+2x^2$","$2+x+6x^2$","$1+2x+6x^3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c955288c108485d8fd33c68d","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A polynomial has the correct value and first derivative at $a$, but the second-order coefficient is $f''(a)$ instead of $f''(a)/2!$. What condition does it fail?","options":["It is no longer a valid polynomial in $x$.","It cannot be centred at $a$.","It generally fails to match $f''(a)$.","It has no constant term."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a0236caaf672f2a0fcf58209","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What data are used to build the $k$-th order Taylor polynomial at $a$?","options":["Only values of $f$ far from $a$","Derivatives of $f$ at $a$ up to order $k$","Only the integral of $f$","Only the zeros of $f$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b3d9ee1b04440ed6691d1676","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"no_diagnostic_value"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the coefficient of $(x-a)^k$ in $P_k(x)$?","options":["$f^{(k)}(x)$","$k!f(a)$","$\\frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!}$","$f'(a)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"740411b6d3952dc12e56a981","time_limit":20,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which is the third-order Taylor polynomial of $\\sin x$ at $0$?","options":["$x-\\frac{x^3}{3!}$","$1+x+\\frac{x^2}{2}$","$x+\\frac{x^3}{3!}$","$\\frac{x^2}{2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"690e888423f230e0a367db4c","time_limit":20,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","questi